{"attack":true,"case":"The target's evolutionary analogy is circular in a way that drains it of justificatory force independently of the induction and pragmatism objections already raised. In the biological formulation, 'survival of the fittest' is tautological when 'fittest' is defined as 'that which survives.' The target replicates this tautology: it says 'survival indicates robustness against error elimination' and that we prefer the 'fittest' (best-corroborated) theory. But 'corroboration' is defined entirely as having survived past tests, and 'robustness' and 'adapted to the problem environment' are introduced as synonyms for that same survival. No independent property is identified that 'fitness,' 'robustness,' or 'adaptedness' pick out beyond the bare fact of having survived. Consequently the analogy cannot justify the preference—it merely re-describes 'the theory that survived tests' as 'the robust/adapted/fit theory' and then says we prefer it because it is 'fit.' The shift from justification to selection therefore changes the vocabulary without changing the content: the analogy adds no reason beyond the brute observation that one theory passed and others did not, which is exactly the datum the preference is supposed to be defended against. A defense whose operative predicate is co-extensive with its conclusion is vacuous.","counterexample":null,"premise":"If the target's key evaluative terms ('fittest,' 'robust,' 'adapted') are defined solely by having survived past tests, then the statement 'we prefer the fittest theory because it is the one known to be adapted' is circular: it asserts we prefer the theory that survived because it is the theory that survived, supplying no independent ground for the preference.","premise_evidence":[{"block":"633287e1b06f5afc8a00c1e12b92fa0f55a9dbf8a0ab5c8c6c6fa3023b5f4036","quote":"Corroboration is not probability but a report of survival in a hostile environment of tests. We prefer the 'fittest' (best-corroborated) theory not because we induce it will survive tomorrow, but because it is the only one currently known to be adapted to the problem environment."}],"successor_question":"Does the target identify any property of 'fitness,' 'robustness,' or 'adaptedness' that is logically independent of having survived past tests, or are these terms co-extensive with corroboration and therefore incapable of grounding the preference non-circularly?"}